// Stashes — work a rabbit stops in the middle of and picks up again later.
//
// A rabbit is an agent you summon to do memory work for you. Burying is memory work, so you tell a
// rabbit to do it: `Have <rabbit> bury <value>.` hands one value out and pauses right there, and
// `unbury` wakes the work up again.
//
// A function that buries anywhere in its body hands back a `stash of <what it buries>` instead of a
// plain value — nothing marks the declaration, exactly as nothing marks a function fallible and
// `return a failure` in the body decides it. What it does need is a rabbit to do the burying, so it
// takes one as a parameter and the caller hands it over.
//
// A stash is not a collection. A series HAS its items; a stash PRODUCES them, one resumption at a
// time, and cannot be counted, indexed or re-read. That is what lets a stash be endless.
//
// When there is nothing left to bury, `unbury` gives back void — so the type of `unbury s` is
// `voidable T`, and asking "is it void?" is how you find out the stash is spent.

// ── Endless is fine ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Nothing here says how many. The loop never ends; it just stops running whenever it buries, and
// only runs again if somebody asks for another one.

Bind number to counting-up, given (the rabbit helper, the number first-value):
    Define next as first-value.
    Repeat:
        Have helper bury next.
        The next becomes next + 1.
    Until false.
Done.

Pull a rabbit as den.
    State "Five, from three:".
    Define counter as cast counting-up on (den, 3).
    Define taken as 0.
    While taken is less than 5, repeat:
        State unbury counter.
        The taken becomes taken + 1.
    Done.

    // ── Every stash has its own place to stand ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
    //
    // Two casts of the same function make two stashes, and neither one knows about the other. The
    // state a rabbit keeps between resumptions belongs to that piece of work, not to the rabbit.

    State "".
    State "A second counter, started later:".
    Define other-counter as cast counting-up on (den, 100).
    State unbury other-counter.
    State unbury counter.
    State unbury other-counter.
    State unbury counter.
Done.

// ── A stash that runs out ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// `Skip` and `Stop` work inside a burying function the way they always do. This one walks a series,
// passes over the short words, and finishes when the series does — after which it is spent, and
// says so by handing back void.

Bind text to long-words-in, given (the rabbit helper, the series of text words):
    For each word in words, repeat:
        If the length of word is less than 4:
            Skip.
        Done.
        Have helper bury word.
    Done.
Done.

// ── A stash is a value ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// It goes wherever a value goes: a parameter, an element of a series, a field, a local inside
// another burying function. That last one is what lets a stash DELEGATE — hold another one and pass
// its values along — which is the difference between a generator and something you can build with.

Bind void to take-three, given (the stash of number source, the text label):
    Define taken as 0.
    While taken is less than 3, repeat:
        State label joined to ": " joined to ((unbury source but void is 0) converted to text).
        The taken becomes taken + 1.
    Done.
Done.

// One stash draining another. `inner` is an ordinary local holding a `stash of number`, and it has
// to survive this function's own resumptions as well as produce values of its own. The rabbit this
// function was handed is the one it gives the inner work to as well.
Bind number to evens-of-counting-up, given (the rabbit helper):
    Define inner as cast counting-up on (helper, 1).
    Repeat:
        Define value as unbury inner but void is 0.
        If value is greater than 8:
            Stop.
        Done.
        If value % 2 is 0:
            Have helper bury value.
        Done.
    Until false.
Done.

Pull a rabbit as warren.
    State "".
    State "Handed to a function:".
    Cast take-three on (cast counting-up on (warren, 7), "seven").

    State "".
    State "Three of them in a series, walked twice:".
    Define many as a series of stash of number.
    Insert (cast counting-up on (warren, 1))   into many.
    Insert (cast counting-up on (warren, 10))  into many.
    Insert (cast counting-up on (warren, 100)) into many.
    For each one-stash in many, repeat:
        State unbury one-stash.
    Done.
    For each one-stash in many, repeat:
        State unbury one-stash.
    Done.

    State "".
    State "One stash draining another:".
    Define evens as cast evens-of-counting-up on (warren).
    Repeat:
        Define value as unbury evens.
        If value is void:
            Stop.
        Done.
        State value.
    Until false.

    State "".
    State "The long words, then void:".
    Define found as cast long-words-in on (warren, a series with ("a", "rabbit", "in", "the", "warren")).
    Repeat:
        Define word as unbury found.
        If word is void:
            Stop.
        Done.
        State word.
    Until false.
    State unbury found.
    State unbury found.
Done.
